nodecode-hashference is a rewrite of 0xalank's 0xalank/hermes-hashference-provider:

  https://github.com/0xalank/hermes-hashference-provider
  commit 82700f5377a46f0e7bd482c907ed04090b6305be
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 0xalank (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the provider profile — the `hashference` id, its OpenAI-compatible
base, its HASHFERENCE_API_KEY and its sign-up page — and the two things that
give the plugin its point, both read from Hashference's public catalog at
`/models`: each model's context is its `max_input_tokens` where stated (not
its larger window), each model's output cap is its
`default_max_output_tokens`, and the picker keeps only what a turn can run on,
which drops the `-messages` aliases, the models the catalog marks as unable to
call tools, and the models that take under 64K input tokens. The plugin's
curated `fallback_models` answer when the catalog is unreachable, and its
per-model lookups (`fetch_models`, `get_model_context_length`,
`get_max_tokens`) are kept. In Nodecode the profile is a row in the models.dev
catalog table, one hook, and a worker that fetches the catalog once at start.

Left out: the Hermes-side plumbing, which has no Nodecode counterpart and no
bearing on what a user gets — the `providers.register_provider` call, the
`ProviderProfile` fields Hermes alone reads (`auth_type`, `hostname`,
`sign_in`), the ten-minute in-process cache (a fetch at start, retried every
ten minutes while unreachable, answers the same for a catalog this stable) and
`default_aux_model`, since Nodecode picks its own auxiliary model. The README's
x402 path — paying per request with a few seconds of proof of work, no account
— was already outside the plugin, because it needs a native RandomX solver, and
stays out here; the repository's own pytest suite and CI are not carried over.
